            {
              "id": "e7ee7c9f-e0b0-3edc-8172-5aec757d189a",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the vulnerability \"Avoid Using Non-Cryptographic Random Number Generators\" refers to the use of functions like `rand()` to generate random numbers. These functions are not suitable for generating random numbers in a security context, as they are predictable and can be easily reproduced. This can lead to various security issues, such as predictability in password generation, session identifiers, or cryptographic keys.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use cryptographic random number generators instead of non-cryptographic ones. In C++, you can use functions from the `<random>` library, such as `std::random_device` or `std::mt19937`.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a code fix for the vulnerability sink:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\n// ...\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_real_distribution<> dis(0, 1);\n\ninputData[i] = dis(gen);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the `<random>` library.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
