              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nIn C++ programming, the \"Avoid Using Weak or Non-Cryptographic Random Number Generators\" vulnerability refers to the use of weak or non-cryptographic random number generators that can lead to predictable or easily guessable random numbers. This can be exploited by attackers to predict the outcome of the random number generation, leading to security vulnerabilities.\n\nThe specific vulnerability sink in question is:\n\n```cpp\nv.size() > RAND_MAX * (size_t) RAND_MAX\n```\n\nThis code is using the `RAND_MAX` constant, which is a part of the C standard library and is used with the `rand()` function. The `rand()` function and `RAND_MAX` constant are considered weak random number generators because they are not suitable for cryptographic purposes.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to use a strong cryptographic random number generator instead of the weak `rand()` function. In C++, the `<random>` library provides several strong random number generators that can be used.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nHere is a recommended fix for the vulnerability sink:\n\n```cpp\n#include <random>\n\nstd::random_device rd;\nstd::mt19937 gen(rd());\nstd::uniform_int_distribution<> dis(1, std::numeric_limits<size_t>::max());\n\nsize_t random_number = dis(gen);\n\nif (v.size() > random_number * random_number) {\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nIn this code, we are using the Mersenne Twister engine (`std::mt19937`) as a strong random number generator. The `std::random_device` is used to seed the random number generator.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code example requires the following library dependencies:\n\n- `<random>`: for the `std::random_device`, `std::mt19937`, and `std::uniform_int_distribution<>` classes.\n- `<limits>`: for the `std::numeric_limits<size_t>::max()` function.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP: Insecure Randomness](https://owasp.org/www-community/vulnerabilities/Insecure_Randomness)\n- [CWE-338: Use of Cryptographically Weak PRNG](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/338.html)"
